  <text>
    <body>
      <div>
        <head>In the directories</head>
        <p>Edmund Ashley, coach builders' ironmonger, is listed from 1878 to 1915 at the corner of <ref target="civic?id=Victoria%20Street">Victoria Street</ref> and <ref target="civic?id=Madeline%20Street">Madeline Street</ref>. In Victoria Street he is the last listing before the Madeline Street corner, at No. 138 from 1890. In Madeline Street he is No. 1 (listed there in 1878 and 1879 and from 1890 to 1915). The listings call him a coach builders' ironmonger (1878 to 1885 and 1905 to 1915), a coach furniture importer (1890), an ironmonger or coachbuilder (1895), and a coachbuilders' materials importer (1900 and 1905).</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <head>Neighbours</head>
        <p>He is next door to the <ref target="civic?id=Royal%20hotel">Royal hotel</ref> throughout. In Madeline Street, J. Rogers's dispensary is at No. 3 in 1878, Henry Jourdain, professor of dancing, is listed next door from 1879 to 1895, and the Ormond Dancing Academy is at No. 3 in 1900. A J. H. Ashley is listed beside him in 1915.</p>
      </div>
